Yatga Undertow

Ingredients: MONGOLIAN YATGA + SHOEGAZE / SLOWCORE + BARITONE GUITAR + PREPARED HARMONIUM + FLOOR TOM / BOWED CYMBAL + BASS CLARINET

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a winter recording residency in a disused hydrometeorological station above Lake Khövsgöl, where yatga player Saruul Erdene was testing long ringing open-string patterns in a concrete observation room while Glasgow guitarist Iain Mercer rehearsed slow, overdriven baritone-guitar chords through a small tube amplifier in the next chamber. The breakthrough came when they noticed that the yatga did not disappear inside distortion if the guitar avoided matching its exact pitch content. Instead, sustained guitar noise could behave like weather around the plucked instrument: a broad harmonic cloud beneath which each yatga note remained individually visible. Prepared harmonium, floor tom, bowed cymbal, and bass clarinet were added to create a style where clean plucked resonance is never replaced by distortion; it survives inside it.

Yatga Undertow is a slow, high-headroom fusion of Mongolian yatga resonance, shoegaze guitar mass, and slowcore pacing. The music rejects the usual world-fusion model of placing a traditional instrument on top of a contemporary rhythm section. The yatga is the structural center. Guitar, reed, harmonium, percussion, and feedback are arranged around the duration of its strings.

The yatga is recorded with a close stereo pair above the plucking field, one ribbon microphone near the lower resonant body, and a distant room microphone positioned several meters away. The close pair captures nail attack, finger damping, string-to-string motion, and sympathetic shimmer. The ribbon adds low wood resonance. The room channel is introduced selectively when the arrangement widens, allowing the same plucked phrase to appear suddenly larger without changing performance intensity.

Players favor open fifths, suspended seconds, narrow pentatonic cells, minor-seventh color, repeating low-high alternations, slow downward figures, struck harmonics, muted ghost plucks, and long decay windows. Fast ornamental display is reduced. A single four-note figure may remain unchanged for thirty-two bars while the guitar environment around it moves from clean air to saturated wall and back again.

The central compositional principle is the Undertow Layer. The yatga establishes a clear foreground contour. Baritone guitar then enters not as accompaniment but as a low, slow-moving harmonic current beneath it. The guitar part often uses different chord inversions, open-string drones, suspended voicings, and filtered distortion so it surrounds the yatga without doubling the plucked notes. When the guitar reaches maximum saturation, the yatga remains dry enough that its attacks still cut through the cloud.

A second principle, Dry Island, protects selected yatga notes from every other sound source. One important pluck may be surrounded by a half-beat or full-beat reduction in guitar, cymbal, harmonium, and reed density. This makes silence around the string as important as distortion around the section. Dry Islands appear irregularly and are never used as predictable drops.

Baritone guitar supplies the principal electric mass. Tunings favor low fifths, suspended voicings, open strings, minor ninth friction, and slowly changing chord fields. Distortion comes from stacked low-gain overdrive, tube compression, controlled feedback, and occasional reverse-reverb pre-bloom. The tone should remain grainy and dimensional rather than fuzzed into featureless white noise. Tremolo picking is sparse. Chords are often allowed to ring for four or eight beats.

Prepared harmonium provides an unstable acoustic pad without becoming cinematic. Selected reeds are lightly damped with removable felt or paper so certain pitches bloom late or decay early. Bellows are worked manually and unevenly. Voicings use fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, and open octaves. The harmonium often appears before the guitar wall enters, providing a fragile acoustic bridge between yatga and electricity.

Floor tom supplies slow physical punctuation rather than a conventional drum groove. Deep center strokes, muted mallet hits, rim knocks, and single low rolls occur every two, four, or eight bars. There is usually no snare. Cymbals are bowed, scraped, or struck only at major structural changes. A conventional rock kit is excluded because constant backbeat would compress the large temporal spaces the genre depends on.

Bass clarinet supplies a dark low-mid current beneath the strings. It uses chalumeau pedals, breath-heavy fundamentals, low fifths, short falling seconds, key noise, and occasional soft multiphonics. It rarely plays when the guitar is at maximum density. Its strongest role is during the transition back to Dry Island, where acoustic low-mid air remains after the electric wall recedes.

Bass guitar is optional. When used, it is baritone-register, lightly overdriven, and extremely sparse: whole notes, fifths, and octave drops only. Most low-frequency weight should come from baritone guitar, floor tom, harmonium, and bass clarinet.

Vocals are optional and distant. Mongolian or English lines may describe visibility reports, frozen gauges, lake depth, wind direction, cloud base, missing weather stations, or the act of counting things that cannot be clearly seen. The vocal is treated as one more layer of atmosphere, not as the center of the arrangement. Lyrics should remain concrete and image-driven rather than abstractly mystical.

Effects are carefully tiered. Yatga remains mostly dry with short room and occasional plate. Guitar uses spring, plate, reverse reverb, tape delay, overdrive, and feedback, but long tails are automated downward immediately before important plucks. Harmonium gets a compact chamber. Bass clarinet gets a dark room. Bowed cymbal may use long reverb only at major section openings. Compression is light enough that the rise and fall of the full arrangement remains obvious.

The defining tension is clarity inside mass. The plucked string never becomes decoration; it is the object the distortion has to move around.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 54–76 BPM, usually 4/4 or slow 6/8, with no constant backbeat. Pulse comes from yatga repetition, baritone-guitar chord changes, isolated floor-tom hits, and long harmonic swells.
- Harmonic Palette: Pentatonic and modal centers, open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, modal thirds, open-string drones, minor-nine friction, and slow chord fields that may remain unchanged for eight or sixteen bars.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Undertow Layer — baritone-guitar distortion moves beneath a dry, clearly articulated yatga foreground instead of doubling it.
  2. Dry Island — selected plucks receive protected negative space where surrounding layers briefly withdraw.
  3. Decay Priority — arrangements are written around the full decay of yatga strings rather than cutting them short for rhythmic density.
  4. Bellows Bridge — prepared harmonium connects acoustic and electric sections through manually uneven sustain.
  5. Low-Drum Scarcity — floor tom marks large structural distances; no constant rock backbeat is allowed.
  6. Feedback Clearance — guitar feedback must duck or thin before important yatga attacks so resonance remains intelligible.
- Dynamics: Intensity grows through wider guitar voicings, thicker feedback, stronger floor-tom weight, longer bowed-cymbal bloom, more active bass-clarinet pedals, and greater room-mic contribution. Climaxes are broad and slow rather than rhythmically busy.

Scene & Origin Lore

Yatga Undertow formed in a retired hydrometeorological station overlooking Lake Khövsgöl. The building retained wind charts, cloud-height tables, analog pressure instruments, cracked observation windows, pale-green cabinets, frost-stiff curtains, steel instrument stands, handwritten lake-temperature logs, and a narrow corridor connecting several acoustically different concrete rooms.

Yatga player Saruul Erdene had been using the station to document how different string dampings behaved in cold, dry air. She became interested in the fact that one clean pluck could remain audible for several seconds after the hand had already moved away.

Glasgow guitarist Iain Mercer was recording slow baritone-guitar drones in the neighboring room. His first attempt at playing directly under Saruul filled every gap and buried the instrument. He then removed most of his notes, lowered his chord voicings, and allowed the guitar amplifier to sustain around rather than through her phrase. The yatga stopped sounding like a guest instrument and became the only clear object inside a large electric weather system.

Prepared-harmonium player Nomin Batsaikhan joined after modifying an old portable reed organ with removable felt strips. Percussionist Callum Reid replaced a full drum kit with one floor tom, one suspended cymbal, and mallets. Bass-clarinetist Oyuna Clarke completed the ensemble with low reed tones that could preserve weight after the guitar wall disappeared.

Their first performances were called visibility readings. A blackboard beside the stage showed only distance values: 10 km, 5 km, 2 km, 500 m, CLEAR. The values did not control electronics. They simply described how much of the yatga the audience should still be able to perceive as the arrangement became denser.

The visual language developed around frost blue, instrument-panel cream, weather-station green, oxidized steel, dark wool, raw wood, black amplifier cloth, and weak tungsten light. The unofficial rule is that no climax is successful if the yatga disappears completely.

