Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Visibility Falls to Two Kilometers
- Story & Context: At 64 BPM, Saruul opens with a four-note yatga cell alternating low tonic, upper fifth, suspended second, and muted return. The phrase repeats unchanged while Nomin introduces a faint prepared-harmonium fifth. Iain enters only after sixteen bars with a low baritone-guitar chord whose upper partials avoid Saruul's exact pitches. Callum strikes one floor-tom note every eight bars. As the track widens, the guitar accumulates overdrive and feedback, but every fourth yatga cycle creates a Dry Island where the guitar tail is pulled down just before the first pluck. Oyuna enters during the final third with one low bass-clarinet pedal. The climax is the loudest guitar field on the EP, but the original four-note yatga cell remains clearly audible through it.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Frosted observation window overlooking a lake reduced to a pale grey horizon, weather board reading VISIBILITY 2 KM, one wooden yatga string sharply in focus while blurred amplifier light glows behind it. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “two kilometers,” “still visible,” “wind from north,” and “count what remains.”

Track 2: Gauge House Keeps One Clear String
- Story & Context: At 72 BPM in slow 6/8, Iain begins with clean baritone-guitar harmonics while Saruul answers with isolated yatga notes separated by full decay. Nomin pumps the harmonium unevenly so one suspended pitch blooms after the others. Callum uses bowed cymbal instead of drum for the first half. Each section adds more guitar saturation, but one designated yatga string is always protected by a Dry Island: every time Saruul strikes it, feedback and harmonium recede for nearly a beat. Oyuna plays short descending bass-clarinet seconds between those protected notes. In the middle, the band reaches near-silence rather than a conventional breakdown, then rebuilds from the same single clear string.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Interior of a small weather gauge house, one clean string drawn horizontally across layers of fogged glass and distorted waveform traces, a pressure dial frozen at one reading. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “one clear string,” “gauge house,” “do not cover,” and “read again.”

Track 3: The Lake Disappears Before the Station
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 56 BPM. There is no floor tom for the opening two minutes. Saruul plays widely spaced descending yatga notes while Nomin sustains a low harmonium fifth. Iain uses volume-swollen baritone chords with almost no pick attack, making the guitar feel like distant weather rather than a foreground instrument. Oyuna adds breath-heavy bass-clarinet fundamentals after every second phrase. When Callum finally enters, he uses one low tom stroke followed by a long bowed-cymbal bloom. The final section gradually removes distortion instead of increasing it. Guitar, cymbal, reed, and harmonium disappear layer by layer until only the original yatga remains. Saruul plays one final low note and lets the full room decay continue into silence.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty frozen observation deck at dusk, the lake horizon erased by dense white mist while the dark weather station remains visible in the foreground, one thin plucked-string waveform fading into blank space. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “lake gone first,” “station remains,” “no horizon,” and “last reading.”

